A||||10||deceler8||Donut spare vs. fix-a-flat...||||1||
Z||000000||deceler8||06-12-2000||12:57 PM||||Everytime I open the hood, I wonder about that nice little area where the spare goes and all the stuff I could put up there.<P>I hate the compact spare because there isn't room for a real tire up there if you do get a flat, and in 20 years of driving, I've only had one flat.<P>Never used Fix a Flat or any of its permutations...Have these improved to the point that you could just carry a can of this and ditch the compact spare...???<P>As to my previous thread regarding A/C compressor installation hints...I'll just assume it's gonna suck 'cause no one replied.<P>Oh well, that's life...some things on this car you just gotta grin and bear.||||reg||1
Z||000001||BN Boomer||06-12-2000||01:02 PM||||I personally wouldn't ditch the spare tire. While Fix-A-Flat is certainly convenient, it does have it's limitations and will not seal large punctures or any damage to the sidewall.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||06-12-2000||07:55 PM||||1. most aerosol flat sealants are extreamly flamable. The use Propane and/or Butane for propelant. They must be stored, used, and purged carefully or fatal explosions can result. Explosions of tires using these products have already killed a number of people.<P>2. Fix a flat is designed to seal only tread punctures from nails screws etc. Punctures under 1/8-3/16inch max depending on the specific brand. It will not fix bead or sidewall problems at all.||||reg||4
Z||000003||lowCG||06-12-2000||10:32 PM||||Do the math,figure the odds,use the fix-a-flat.I've had two flats in fifteen years myself.<BR>A small 12V air compressor isn't a bad idea either,and I do carry a jack,lugwrench.||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||06-12-2000||10:44 PM||||You guys must be living right or something. I have at least one or two flats on my vehicles each year, but I also drive close to 40K miles a year. No way would I leave home without a spare. That fix-a-flat crap is bad for the tires and I can't afford a new tire everytime one goes flat just because I'm too lazy to change it or because I want to use the spare tire space for something else. Just my opinion.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ed||06-13-2000||01:07 AM||||When the Ferrari F-40 came out, there wasn't a spare.  Only a can of sealant.  <P>I suppose if you've got an F-40, you've also got a friend who can travel (far) behind you with a front and rear spare in their truck!  <P>The Ferrari 250 GTO had a tire which would only fit the front, because the rear tire was too big for the "trunk."  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||06-13-2000||01:34 AM||||Ed, I think you have the wrong Forum. This is the Fiero Forum, not the Ferrari Forum...LOL.||||reg||1
Z||000007||86se/lambo||06-13-2000||01:57 AM||||I've used the fix-a flat and its worked great. personally i'll keep both in there, just in case ... (like if the jack wont fit)<BR>good point on the ferrari ed, always good tgo have a different perspective. nothing wrong with diggin ferrari and fieros.<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000008||phillip||06-13-2000||09:13 AM||||both. fix a flat's fast and convienient when you can get away with it ( i think you just have to tell whoever repairs your tire that it's in there) but a spare covers all the bases (except multi-flats, but then you might seal one and change another.)  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||ChadMan||06-13-2000||09:18 AM||||I have heard that the cheap-o brands of tire inflaters/sealers can corrode your wheels, but that the better name brands (like Prestone's Tire Jack) won't.<P>When my wife and I took the Coupe to Florida in '98, I bought some Tire Jack and left the spare at home, just because we needed the space.  We never needed it (thank heaven) so I can't comment on how well it does or doesn't work at fixing flats.||||reg||1
Z||000010||olympic||06-13-2000||02:45 PM||||   Tire sealants are OK, they're just not a permanent fix.  You need to drive straight to a tire shop and have it fixed permanently.<BR>   Other options include Goodyear "runflats" and those other ones that seal punctures automatically. ||||reg||1
